{"schemaVersion":1,"sourceRecord":{"schemaVersion":1,"recordId":"wikibooks-466903-r4629567","sourceId":"wikibooks-cookbook-cc-by-sa","sourceTitle":"Cookbook:Acevichada Sauce","sourcePageId":466903,"sourceRevision":"4629567","sourceRevisionTimestamp":"2026-04-08T06:38:45Z","sourceRevisionUser":"A Sulaiman Z","sourceUrl":"https://en.wikibooks.org/w/index.php?title=Cookbook:Acevichada%20Sauce&oldid=4629567","retrievedAt":"2026-10-13T12:17:17.776Z","sourceCategories":[],"license":{"id":"wikibooks-cookbook-cc-by-sa-4.0","name":"Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-SA 4.0); Wikibooks text is generally dual-licensed with GFDL unless otherwise noted","evidenceUrl":"https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Wikibooks:Copyrights","policyStatus":"verified-license","pageExceptionStatus":"not-reviewed-per-page","attribution":"Attribute Wikibooks contributors by linking each version-specific source page. Indicate that structured fields are extracted and any translated or edited text is modified. Include CC BY-SA 4.0 notice/link and share adaptations under CC BY-SA 4.0 or later.","changeNoticeRequired":true,"shareAlikeRequired":true},"sourceSha256":"435d9ac1dee00c4ebce0fb93ac6e6db423d11fe7b0b6fb5211bf9892adbafe61","sourceWikitext":"__NOTOC__{{Recipe summary\n| Category = Sauce recipes\n| Cuisine = Peruvian\n| Yield = About 750 ml\n| Servings = About 6\n| Time = 30 minutes\n| Difficulty = 3\n}}\n\n{{Recipe}}[[File:Nitrogen Frozen Tuna Acevichado Maki.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Acevichado sauce as a topping of ''uramakizushi''.]][[File:Ceviche Hot Roll.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Acevichado sauce as a topping of hot roll]]\n\n'''Acevichado sauce''' is a recent contribution to Peruvian cuisine that highlights the country's blend of several cultures, including the Japanese diaspora at the end of the 19th century. The recipe is said to have originated during the 90s in Lima's Matsuei restaurant, which is famous for the creation of the iconic acevichado maki. The sauce, which is sometimes described as a \"Leche de Tigre mayonnaise\", references the Peruvian style of ''uramakizushi'' and has undergone several changes to this day. It is commonly used as a Nikkei-style sushi topping, but more recently, it is used for dipping mixed seafood fritters (jalea de mariscos) and even pizza or chicken wings!\n\n== Ingredients ==\n* 100 [[Cookbook:Milliliter|ml]] key [[Cookbook:Lime|lime]] juice (from 8–10 limes)\n* 20 [[Cookbook:Gram|g]] sea [[Cookbook:Salt|salt]]\n* 20 g red [[Cookbook:Onion|onions]]\n* 20 g [[Cookbook:Aji Amarillo|ají amarillo]] chile\n* 10 g [[Cookbook:Aji Limo|ají limo]] chile\n* 10 g [[Cookbook:Garlic|garlic]]\n* 5 g [[Cookbook:Cilantro|cilantro]] leaves\n* 5 g hondashi (Japanese [[Cookbook:Dehydrated Broth|dehydrated broth]]) \n* 5 g [[Cookbook:Pepper|black pepper]] powder\n* 5 g togarashi pepper powder\n* 375 ml [[Cookbook: Vegetable Oil|soybean oil]]\n* 2 pasteurized [[Cookbook:Egg|eggs]]\n\n== Procedure ==\n\n=== Leche de tigre ===\n\n# Pour the lime juice into a bowl.\n# Add the salt and test the flavor. It should be neither too salty nor too acidic. If needed, rectify by adding more salt or lime juice to find the right balance.\n# Add the onions, chiles, garlic, cilantro, hondashi, black pepper, and togarashi.\n# Cover with [[Cookbook:Plastic Wrap|plastic wrap]] and reserve in the fridge for at least 15 minutes, so the flavors from the vegetables and spices can integrate into the lime juice.\n\n=== Mayonnaise ===\n\n# Crack the eggs into a [[Cookbook:Blender|blender]], and start blending at low speed.\n# While running the blender, very gradually start adding 250 ml of the oil until the mixture thickens to a mayonnaise consistency. Use more or less oil as needed.\n# Pause the blender, pour your ''leche de tigre'' into the mix, and start blending again.\n# Gradually stream in the remaining 125 ml oil until the mix thickens again.\n# Turn off the blender and taste for salt. If needed, add a little more salt, and blend again until it suits your taste.\n\n== Storage ==\nKeep the finished sauce in the fridge, where it should last for approximately 2 days.\n\n[[Category:Recipes using lime juice]]\n[[Category:Recipes using salt]]\n[[Category:Recipes using onion]]\n[[Category:Recipes using aji amarillo]]\n[[Category:Recipes using garlic]]\n[[Category:Recipes using cilantro]]\n[[Category:Recipes using pepper]]\n[[Category:Recipes using egg]]\n[[Category:Recipes for sauces and condiments]]\n[[Category:Peruvian recipes]]\n[[Category:Recipes for mayonnaise]]","isRecipe":false,"recipe":{"name":"Acevichada Sauce","nameAr":null,"servings":null,"servingsSourceText":"About 6","ingredients":[{"id":"source-line-1","name":"100 ml key lime juice (from 8–10 limes)","sourceText":"100 ml key lime juice (from 8–10 limes)","amountText":"100 ml key lime juice (from 8–10 limes)","amount":null,"unit":null,"foodBinding":null},{"id":"source-line-2","name":"20 g sea salt","sourceText":"20 g sea salt","amountText":"20 g sea salt","amount":null,"unit":null,"foodBinding":null},{"id":"source-line-3","name":"20 g red onions","sourceText":"20 g red onions","amountText":"20 g red onions","amount":null,"unit":null,"foodBinding":null},{"id":"source-line-4","name":"20 g ají amarillo chile","sourceText":"20 g ají amarillo chile","amountText":"20 g ají amarillo chile","amount":null,"unit":null,"foodBinding":null},{"id":"source-line-5","name":"10 g ají limo chile","sourceText":"10 g ají limo chile","amountText":"10 g ají limo chile","amount":null,"unit":null,"foodBinding":null},{"id":"source-line-6","name":"10 g garlic","sourceText":"10 g garlic","amountText":"10 g garlic","amount":null,"unit":null,"foodBinding":null},{"id":"source-line-7","name":"5 g cilantro leaves","sourceText":"5 g cilantro leaves","amountText":"5 g cilantro leaves","amount":null,"unit":null,"foodBinding":null},{"id":"source-line-8","name":"5 g hondashi (Japanese dehydrated broth)","sourceText":"5 g hondashi (Japanese dehydrated broth)","amountText":"5 g hondash